#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105383 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "dcd503a8-a9c6-524b-a8ac-0641ce3e1107" :question/math-academy-id "e-12423" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,2), (0,1)$ and $(1,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T?$\n\n- $(-2,0)$\n\n- $(0,0)$\n\n- $(2,0)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-2,0),$ $(0,0),$ and $(2,0).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II,  and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105384 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "964efb4a-6e6b-5fc1-a993-20aa2b0acdaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-12424" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n  Find the image of the triangle shown below under the action of $\\mathbf T.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/853fd7d5c09944a5bacd0c2b3453504f3fa1eea9d8d3b2c3bdd4f0f44889dc16.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(3,-6), (1,-2),$ and $(-2,4).$  Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/3fd1073b2eaa5aa9c21e537fcc4dc6cf538ab2992c0732f64da50dbcb63d8fd4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105385 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "4aa087bf-ee6c-5e90-9431-c4be6b207333" :question/math-academy-id "e-12438" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/89efcd0ecb51dac08a1102c5a347eea3459f7209de7cbac0a88b192259ca3b7e.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $\\mathbf{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with associated $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., $\\det(T) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., $\\det(T) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the origin."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ca82933-661e-5f97-a835-1e5e885d7be0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/4966000e14a3ed4070b9875848c52ae6a4e3e7d01d15aaf312ae0a2119daf042.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284105} {:answer/id #uuid "e915a293-6110-5947-a4a9-7d503ede6513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/fee3415af228b80bb5d7c1a68475043fce324234766d243c928fe9eb98e0b900.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284106} {:answer/id #uuid "1eca9c50-5358-5174-a0b1-dbd0fc8b6168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/cf336b4522756131aeb6dd9bc48ada6f8b255526b6ae20120dcf40f155b1b95c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284107} {:answer/id #uuid "2b76b6d8-da41-567c-88b1-217a3d69c2b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/e997a8908870618b2aea4d1461c8fd719a4fc1ba0fde1c025a830abeed53aac8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284108} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ad24bb-3a2c-5fc7-8d26-c836599a24cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/bd92e2e12164033952a9b4c6233563af86ef6b9add13b66700ab166ff04821a1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1eca9c50-5358-5174-a0b1-dbd0fc8b6168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/cf336b4522756131aeb6dd9bc48ada6f8b255526b6ae20120dcf40f155b1b95c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284107} :answer-field/id #uuid "1160794a-84c7-5140-bbc1-ded3cd0dfaa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7076e203-2050-53c1-bc3c-efab42cd73e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-147649" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/57941a3ed467617de9d41a2d8239b75060ba90b7a8c8980e5dabd3afb0be8b6b.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-1,1)$, $(-5,5),$ $(-3,3).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/cf336b4522756131aeb6dd9bc48ada6f8b255526b6ae20120dcf40f155b1b95c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284110 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f35c6757-c1cc-5e95-adc9-7f3b43987bee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284112} {:answer/id #uuid "f3aab5aa-276e-5326-bbfe-4b9e1ca11ad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284113} {:answer/id #uuid "58ad1e6b-adf3-52cd-aecb-ac30e59feb28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284114} {:answer/id #uuid "cda1164d-4de5-56fe-ab02-1b31eb5243b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284115} {:answer/id #uuid "73c06a27-23a8-5503-bac0-b74613d69742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3aab5aa-276e-5326-bbfe-4b9e1ca11ad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284113} :answer-field/id #uuid "be65ef3d-5052-5673-a3d9-6e70ca192993" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88e719bc-e349-5ca0-a13c-a121f56b9351" :question/math-academy-id "q-147668" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(1,0)$, $(0,-3)$ and $(-2,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(12,-24)$\n- $(8,16)$\n- $(2,-4)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 12 & -8 \\\\ -4 & -24 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(2,-4)$, $(12,-24)$ and $(-8,16).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee5af7d6-ac78-5d00-8c37-da4bcfdf7a70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284119} {:answer/id #uuid "820502db-e4d6-55f6-9fdc-553dfcad78b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284120} {:answer/id #uuid "838c1b7a-be1e-5ec4-b336-551b12185475" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284121} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee21e47-6f22-5717-8570-01e475c9354a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284122} {:answer/id #uuid "635d14e7-39d4-5aa7-b218-5ecef799c9bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee5af7d6-ac78-5d00-8c37-da4bcfdf7a70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284119} :answer-field/id #uuid "41075aa9-0276-571b-a372-d1c938d5d900" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6364a192-b48f-5703-8297-b5247d60439d" :question/math-academy-id "q-147671" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ 10 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,-1),(0,2),$ and $(-2,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(6,-1)$\n- $(7,-14)$\n- $(8,-16)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ 10 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -6 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 12 & -14\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(8,-16),$ $(-6,12)$, and $(7,-14).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96613aa1-9c36-500a-a41e-c35cb51432aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102200} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf8242c-8fb2-5898-ad17-c871c5f73bf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102201} {:answer/id #uuid "ce929ac1-17f1-5017-a524-3020f0f83977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102202} {:answer/id #uuid "e7aa19f6-551c-5953-ba44-dd77177a2953" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102203} {:answer/id #uuid "fb67936e-1532-50e7-8865-84bea44bb18c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7aa19f6-551c-5953-ba44-dd77177a2953" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102203} :answer-field/id #uuid "699e658e-8932-5996-9d7e-35e0ceaaf3fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6dc04420-7edf-5061-a2f1-81e1e58efd27" :question/math-academy-id "q-162145" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/e2ab6906100a96a123b4b1520613f1e38fe2c7e77af390a8efe0596a2861727d.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a line segment."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d221da97-895b-554e-a0d0-4530a8bc7dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284126} {:answer/id #uuid "1fe16775-3759-5a79-97ca-1f38d17cc42c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284127} {:answer/id #uuid "d703169c-1e19-58cf-a9d1-b6cc5d645446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pentagon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284128} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ada3c1-d523-5656-bdd8-ea73b1f354ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284129} {:answer/id #uuid "5da67961-9e35-5789-b3b9-477442337571" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d221da97-895b-554e-a0d0-4530a8bc7dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284126} :answer-field/id #uuid "7db89d2d-1270-5238-a5d8-8337010a987f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f5f7e14-de4a-5b80-a0ab-301a0dcb6346" :question/math-academy-id "q-162199" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/eb1247ddcaaf12a342aa036550ed4f9f7daaeb6037e7ebbab0de619bb6975dbe.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the pentagon shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b61b27bc-e86f-5025-90fe-510e1fecf08f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284133} {:answer/id #uuid "f10a5988-93ff-50e6-aac5-4bc31c71115c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284134} {:answer/id #uuid "46dd404a-75be-562a-ac29-e454cdd77c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284135} {:answer/id #uuid "53fb57c9-9633-5910-ac73-62b028f87b04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284136} {:answer/id #uuid "65aa1cb7-8757-560f-972d-1ce4bf7ca90b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b61b27bc-e86f-5025-90fe-510e1fecf08f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284133} :answer-field/id #uuid "510e7c4c-1573-5703-b264-e1e135f71bf7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b068ccdf-2483-5d2b-9c0a-f13dae9811af" :question/math-academy-id "q-162200" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/da6c0fb32e62b6effe133c527fee528c47b6f1c8dc7dd1d77d7d04f42b4c3d2b.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284138 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4437aa5a-a749-5c6d-8f3d-d31b01601abb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284140} {:answer/id #uuid "f85eb3db-c70b-5e67-bd54-ecfc8961e9fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284141} {:answer/id #uuid "214d698e-c823-548f-ae58-51ab4fe4e6f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284142} {:answer/id #uuid "8fd42b68-f6b9-5024-b293-d78a5df92981" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284143} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd14e6c-96a8-5e25-8afa-